(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-3-gram-negative-bacilli-quiz"]={"title":"Gram-Negative Bacilli — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How is a Pseudomonas cell flagellated?","choices":["Peritrichous flagella","Not flagellated","A tuft at both ends","One polar flagellum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: pseudomonads are small Gram-negative rods with a single polar flagellum, which also helps cells in biofilms adhere. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 14, 17 and 71 and image on Slide 15"},{"q":"Why is Pseudomonas aeruginosa a frequent contaminant of ventilators, intravenous solutions and anesthesia equipment?","choices":["It forms endospores","It needs rich nutrients","It lives inside amebas","It forms biofilms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: its ability to produce biofilms lets it contaminate ventilators, intravenous solutions and anesthesia equipment; cells in biofilms adhere by flagella and pili and form microcolonies. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 17, 16 and 39"},{"q":"Which kind of vaccine prevents Haemophilus influenzae type b disease?","choices":["An oral four-dose vaccine","An attenuated vaccine","A subunit vaccine","An acellular toxoid vaccine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the vaccine against Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) is a subunit vaccine. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 98, 29, 31 and 74"},{"q":"In toxigenic diarrheal disease, what do the bacteria cause at the intestinal epithelium?","choices":["Perforation of the wall","Destruction of microvilli","Oversecretion of fluid","Invasion with bleeding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: in toxigenic disease (example: enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli) bacteria on the epithelium cause an oversecretion of fluids; in invasive disease (example: Shigella) they enter the tissue and cause bleeding. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 43, 58 and 74 and image on Slide 44"},{"q":"Which vaccines give temporary protection against typhoid fever?","choices":["A vaccine given to animals","An oral and an injected vaccine","An acellular toxoid vaccine","A subunit capsule vaccine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: two vaccines give temporary protection, an oral vaccine (4 doses) and an injected vaccine (1 dose). — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 74, 28, 31 and 98"},{"q":"Which measure is given to prevent enteropathogenic Escherichia coli infection?","choices":["Breast feeding","A Haemophilus subunit vaccine","Quarantine and rodent control","Pasteurizing dairy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: breast feeding is the prevention given for this wasting infantile diarrhea; treatment is fluid and electrolyte therapy. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 58, 27, 90 and 98"},{"q":"Serratia marcescens pneumonia is typical in which hosts?","choices":["Unvaccinated infants","Chronic alcoholics","Cystic fibrosis patients","Contact lens wearers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Serratia marcescens causes pneumonia in chronic alcoholics, as well as burn and wound infections, urinary tract infections, and septicemia and meningitis in immunocompromised hosts. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 68, 18, 19, 22 and 35"},{"q":"Which stage of pertussis brings a recurrent persistent cough followed by a deep inhalation, the whoop?","choices":["Catarrhal","Septicemic","Pneumonic","Paroxysmal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: in the paroxysmal stage, a recurrent persistent cough is followed by a deep inhalation, the whoop; the catarrhal stage before it brings nasal drainage, congestion, sneezing and an occasional cough. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 31 and 88"},{"q":"Which foods transmit Escherichia coli O157:H7?","choices":["Contaminated beef, water, vegetables","Raw oysters and other shellfish","Honey given to young infants","Eggs from backyard flocks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: from its reservoir in cattle intestines, Escherichia coli O157:H7 reaches people through contaminated beef, water and vegetables. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 60, 76 and 77"},{"q":"Which mediator triggered by endotoxin acts as a vasodilator in shock?","choices":["Bradykinin","Fibrin","Collagenase","Colony stimulating factor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: endotoxin may trigger production of bradykinin, a vasodilator in shock, alongside its coagulation effects such as disseminated intravascular coagulation. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 9 and 10"},{"q":"With which organisms does Legionella pneumophila live in water systems?","choices":["Amebas","Fleas","Turtles","Cattle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Legionella is widely distributed in water (tap water, cooling towers, spas, ponds, air conditioning, supermarket vegetable sprayers) and lives in close association with amebas. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 39, 60, 77 and 83 and image on Slide 41"},{"q":"On which medium is Legionella pneumophila grown?","choices":["Eosin methylene blue agar","Chocolate agar of cooked blood","MacConkey's bile salt agar","Charcoal yeast extract agar"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Legionella cannot be easily cultured; its growth is shown on charcoal yeast extract agar. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 39, 48 and 97 and image on Slide 41"},{"q":"What makes MacConkey's agar selective for Gram-negative bacteria?","choices":["Heated, cooked blood cells","Crystal violet and bile salts","Eosin and methylene blue","Charcoal and yeast extract"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: MacConkey's agar selects for Gram-negative bacteria with crystal violet and bile salts and is differential for lactose fermenters. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 48, 41 and 97"},{"q":"What distinguishes sylvatic plague from urban plague?","choices":["Bubonic versus pneumonic illness","Flea bite versus inhaled aerosol","Western versus eastern states","Wild versus domestic animal source"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: humans develop plague through contact with wild animals (sylvatic plague) or with domestic or semidomestic animals (urban plague), or, rarely, infected humans. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 83 and 88"},{"q":"How is melioidosis generally acquired?","choices":["A tick or flea bite","Unpasteurized dairy products","Undercooked ground beef","Penetrating injury or inhalation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Burkholderia pseudomallei lives in the soil and water of tropical climates, and melioidosis is generally acquired through a penetrating injury or by inhalation. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 23, 27 and 60 and image on Slide 24"},{"q":"Which form of plague is highly contagious by aerosol?","choices":["Sylvatic","Pneumonic","Bubonic","Septicemic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: in pneumonic plague the infection is localized to the lungs, highly contagious by aerosol infection, and fatal without treatment. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 83 and 88 and image on Slide 84"},{"q":"How does Shigella cause dysentery?","choices":["It perforates the bowel wall","It sticks to small-bowel cells","It invades the large-bowel villi","It spreads into the bloodstream"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Shigella invades the villi of the large intestine and enters Peyer's patches to set off inflammation; it does not perforate the intestine or invade the blood. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 78, 56 and 74"},{"q":"Which rash marks typhoid fever?","choices":["An ulcer with swollen nodes","A follicular skin rash","Rose-colored spots","Purpura with necrosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: typhoid fever brings a sustained fever (103-104 degrees), a rash of rose-colored spots, weakness, stomach pains and loss of appetite. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 74, 19, 29 and 88"},{"q":"How do the toxins of enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli cause diarrhea?","choices":["Fluid and electrolyte secretion","Mechanical damage of the wall","Destruction of microvilli","Apoptosis of renal tubule cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the heat-labile and heat-stable toxins promote secretion of fluid and electrolytes in the intestinal epithelium, giving diarrhea, cramping and a low-grade to no fever. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 56, 57, 58 and 60"},{"q":"Which Haemophilus species causes chancroid?","choices":["Haemophilus ducreyi","Haemophilus aegyptius","Haemophilus influenzae","Haemophilus parainfluenzae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Haemophilus ducreyi causes chancroid, a sexually transmitted disease seen primarily in the tropics and primarily in males. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 100 and 98"},{"q":"Which opportunist has multiple animal reservoirs and causes catheter-related urinary tract infections?","choices":["Klebsiella pneumoniae","Haemophilus ducreyi","Providencia species","Pasteurella multocida"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Providencia has multiple animal reservoirs and causes nosocomial infections, urinary tract infections with catheters, and gastroenteritis. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 72, 66, 96 and 100"},{"q":"Which complication of enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli infection damages the kidneys?","choices":["Rose-spot enteric fever","Hemolytic uremic syndrome","Bladder and kidney stones","Infective endocarditis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: in a minority of cases, toxins produce apoptosis of renal tubular epithelial cells and coagulation, the hemolytic uremic syndrome. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 60, 59, 70, 74 and 100"},{"q":"Which extraintestinal infection does Escherichia coli cause most often, more frequently in women?","choices":["Swimmer's ear infection","Urinary tract infection","Infective endocarditis","Acute conjunctivitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Escherichia coli causes most urinary tract infections, more frequently in women and in catheterized patients; it also causes neonatal meningitis, pneumonia, septicemia and wound infections. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 65, 19 and 100"},{"q":"By which routes is tularemia transmitted?","choices":["Contaminated hospital equipment","Animals, water, dust or vector bites","Mainly from person to person","Unpasteurized milk and cheese"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: tularemia is transmitted by contact with infected animals, by water and dust, or by the bites of vectors. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 29, 17, 26 and 27"},{"q":"Which pair of findings marks enteroinvasive Escherichia coli infection?","choices":["Bloody diarrhea and high fever","Watery diarrhea, little fever","Wasting diarrhea in infants","Rose spots and sustained fever"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: it causes profuse bloody diarrhea and high fever, from inflammatory disease of the large intestine; the very young and very old are most susceptible. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 57, 56, 58 and 74"},{"q":"What is the reservoir of Bordetella pertussis?","choices":["The intestines of cattle","Water cooling towers","Apparently healthy carriers","Rodents and their fleas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the reservoir of pertussis is apparently healthy carriers, from whom it spreads by direct contact or aerosols. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 30, 39, 60 and 83"},{"q":"Why might a Pseudomonas aeruginosa vaccine harm a patient with cystic fibrosis?","choices":["It is delivered through the gut","It may worsen lung inflammation","No candidate vaccine exists","It causes the infection itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: because patients with cystic fibrosis often have a chronic infection, vaccination may induce increased pulmonary inflammation and tissue damage. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which test is used to diagnose brucellosis?","choices":["An agglutination titer","Rainbow agar culture","Bubo aspiration","Charcoal yeast extract agar"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: brucellosis is tested with a serological test, the agglutination titer test. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 28, 41, 64 and 90"},{"q":"Which measures prevent plague?","choices":["Breast feeding infants","Boosters for adolescents","Pasteurizing dairy products","Quarantine and rodent control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: plague is prevented by quarantine and control of the rodent population in human habitats; a killed or attenuated vaccine is available for military personnel, veterinarians and laboratory workers. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, Slide 90, 27, 36 and 58"},{"q":"In the scheme for Gram-negative pathogens, which genus is the obligate anaerobe?","choices":["Escherichia","Haemophilus","Bacteroides","Pseudomonas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the obligate anaerobes branch of the Gram-negative rods holds Bacteroides; the aerobes include Pseudomonas, and the facultative anaerobes include Escherichia and Haemophilus. — Lecture 14  Dr. Webster Gram Negative Bacilli of Medical Importance.pptx, image on Slide 12"}]};
